 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 511313,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Graph the line of the equation <span data-math=\"y =  - x - 3\">y =  - x - 3</span> using its slope and <span data-math=\"y\">y</span>-intercept.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>When the equation is in the slope-intercept form of the equation of the line,</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"y = mx + b\"></span>.</p>\n<p><i>m</i> = the slope.</p>\n<p>(0, <i>b</i>) = the y-intercept.</p>\n<table class=\"unnumbered unstyled\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span data-math=\"y = - x - 3\"></span></td>\n<td>This is in slope intercept form.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The slope is –1. To give you a rise over run, write this as <span data-math=\"\\frac{{ - 1}}{1}\"></span>.</p>\n<p>The <i>y</i>-intercept is (0, –3).</p>\n<p>Plot the point (0, –3).</p>\n<p>From that point, use the slope <span data-math=\"\\left( {m = \\frac{{rise}}{{run}} = \\frac{{ - 1}}{1}} \\right)\"></span> to go down one, then right one to plot the next point.</p>\n<p>Connect the two points with a line.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<img src=\"https://exercises.openstax.org/rails/active_storage/blobs/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsibWVzc2FnZSI6IkJBaHBBZ1VOIiwiZXhwIjpudWxsLCJwdXIiOiJibG9iX2lkIn19--632543a2a387cee2bc74567b0bd7fd4721d5e7c6/CS_Figure_05_08_018.png\" alt=\"A line is plotted on an x y coordinate plane. The x and y axes range from negative 10 to 10, in increments of 1. The line passes through the following points, (negative 10, 7), (negative 3, 0), (0, negative 3), (1, negative 4), and (6, negative 9). Note: all values are approximate.\">"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
